tool stdout: {"content":"# Next prompt ideas\n\nWhen things become complicated, remember the principle of divide and conquer.\n\n---\n\nThe menu the user reaches by pressing ESC, where they can find the history of previous sessions, or start a new one. One an item is selected, it does not leave the menu. Which is unexpected. Also it seems like Ctrl+N is not working to start a new session, which I think it should do.\n\n---\n\nThe agent-runner session log should be base on date and time, plus possibly the first few words of the original prompt, if that's available. Keywords would have been nice, but that's likely too complicated.\n\n---\n\nThe background colors that are applied on the output, must always extend to the full width of the screen, only exclusing the border. We want the background to look a bit like a textbox that encapsulates it, without the need of border characters. This should only be applied to larger text blocks and not to single line output like the tool use strings. Those can share the agent result output background. Also make sure this is used sensibly and not in a way that distracts or make things hard to read. I.e. always make sure we have enough contrast between the text color and the background. Differences in background color should be noticable, but quite subtle, as a way to encapsulate larger text blocks only. Please consider what is aesthetically pleasing and makes for a nice, modern, experience. Thanks\n\n---\n\nThe prompt limit is far too low, whatever that is. We want to be able to run very long prompts without issue. It is important that they can run uninterrupted. The tooling should continue until they reach a result, or detect that the agent is hung/looping, or otherwise fail to comply with the request.\n\n---\n\nIn the `status` command output, the `Action:` field value is not aligned with\nthe start position of the values for the other fields.\n\n\nIn the `status` we currently have this entry, where you can see the `action` issue above. However there are other issues too. The reason seems to be truncated, which is not nice. Neither is the reason, which does not seem to be caused by the task, but is rather a limitation in the agent executor. Therefore it seems unreasonable to mark it as `blocked` because the agent execution failed. Wouldn't it be more reasonable to leave it as in-progress or something instead?\n```\n  Component: sandbox_runner\n    State:     blocked\n    Documents: all valid (5/5)\n    Tasks:     4/6 completed (66%) [1 pending, 1 blocked]\n    Action: Resolve the blocked tasks below; 'kvist help task-states' explains each state and its resolution.\n    Blocked:   sandbox-runner-reverification-security-audit\n               reason: agent execution exceeded the combined output limit while contacting the local model\n               gateway.…\n               next:   kvist task run sandbox_runner sandbox-runner-reverification-security-audit\n```\n\n---\n\nIn the `agent-runner` output, I'd like to have color-coded background for different type of content, to differentiate it a bit more.\n\n---\n\nWhat can we learn or get inspired by from either of [Goose](https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose) or [OpenCode](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode), which have at partial Rust implementations. I guess we cannot use either directly since we aim for a more secure wrapping of the agent execution. However, surely there are things that we can pick up from them and be inspired by. They may have much better handling than we do in some areas. Perhaps they have other functionality or features that we could benefit from too. Or perhaps there are parts that we can adopt wholesale? Do an in-depth comparison between these alternatives. Then write down your findings in a Markdown document. Please investigate, ponder and advise. Thanks\n\nHere's what Gemini had to say about it:\n```\n1. Concrete Things to Import from the Rust Ecosystem\n\nRather than reinventing common agentic sub-components, several battle-tested Rust crates and designs from tools like Goose (aaif-goose/goose), Aider, and the broader Rust terminal ecosystem can be integrated directly:\nA. Structured To","next_offset":4096,"offset":0,"sha256":"sha256:cb2bcbd48190891220fe66e21b51e4933fdaaad99b453d08cd66b95264620e34","total_bytes":11459}

